appgen generated this REST API from the app definition `{{ app.name }}`,
with the FastAPI binding. Do not edit the generated files: change the app
definition, and run `appgen generate` again. Write your own code in the
extension files (`*_ext.py`), which appgen creates once and never
changes:

- `<operation>_ext.py`: the logic of a custom operation. Until you write
  it, the endpoint gives 501.
{% if py.entities.values() | map(attribute="queries") | select | list %}
- `<query>_ext.py`: all rows of a query, in their order (ADR 0045). The
  endpoint returns one page of them. Until you write it, the endpoint
  gives 501.
{% endif %}
- `validation_ext.py`: the rules that the create and update operations
  check before they store a record. By default, it accepts all records.
- `app/extensions/routers_ext.py`: the routers of the endpoints that the
  definition does not describe. `app/main.py` includes each router of
  `ROUTERS` after the generated routers. By default, the list is empty.
{% if py_webhooks %}
- `app/webhooks/<webhook>/handler_ext.py`: the logic of a webhook. Until
  you write it, the endpoint gives 501.
{% endif %}

When you write the code of an extension file, change `@trace:gap` to
`@trace:manual` in its header.

## Stack

- FastAPI 0.161 on Python 3.14
- Vertical slice architecture
- SQLAlchemy 2 (async), Alembic, and Postgres (SQLite for local runs)
- uv

## Commands

| Task               | Command                                   |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| Install            | `uv sync`                                 |
| Migrate the database | `uv run alembic upgrade head`           |
| Add the seed records | `uv run python -m app.cli seed`         |
| Start the API      | `uv run fastapi dev app/main.py`          |
{% if py.jobs %}
| Run the jobs       | `uv run python -m app.worker`             |
{% endif %}
| Lint               | `uv run ruff check`                       |
| Check the format   | `uv run ruff format --check`              |
| Type check         | `uv run pyright`                          |
| Test               | `uv run pytest`                           |
| Run with Docker    | `docker compose up --build`               |

The API documentation is at `/docs` when the API runs. The API reads
its settings from environment variables or from a `.env` file (see
`.env.example`), for example `DATABASE_URL`.

## Local database

To run the API without a Postgres server, use SQLite. Write the URL
into `.env`, then migrate the database and start the API:

```sh
printf 'DATABASE_URL=sqlite+aiosqlite:///./local.db\n' > .env
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run python -m app.cli seed
uv run fastapi dev app/main.py
```

The API and Alembic read `DATABASE_URL` from `.env` or from an
exported environment variable. A shell variable that you do not export
has no effect.

## Seed data

`uv run python -m app.cli seed` adds the seed records to the database
of `DATABASE_URL`. Apply the migrations first. The records come from
the app definition, and are the same on each run. The seed adds each
record of the `seed` section of the definition that the database does
not have (by ID), and does not change a record that it has. It adds the
generated mock records only if all tables are empty. With
`DATABASE_SEED=true`, the API runs the seed when it starts, as
`docker compose up` does. `app/core/seed_data.py` holds the records.
{% if definition.auth and definition.auth.password %}
With `SEED_PASSWORD`, each new seed user can sign in with this password.
{% endif %}

The database is the file `local.db`. The models and the migrations work
on both databases. Use Postgres in production: SQLite has one writer at
a time, and it stores the date and time values in UTC.

## Schema changes

appgen creates the first migration (`migrations/versions/0001_schema.py`)
once, and a new run of appgen keeps all migrations. To change the schema
of a database that has data:

1. Change the app definition, and generate the app again into this
   folder.
2. Make the next migration from the changed models. Alembic compares
   the models with the database of `DATABASE_URL`:

   ```sh
   uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add the phone of a person"
   ```

3. Review the new file in `migrations/versions/`. Alembic does not find
   all changes: edit the migration for a rename (Alembic gives a drop
   and an add) or for a changed enum value.
4. Apply it: `uv run alembic upgrade head`. The data stays.
{% if py.full_text %}

The full-text search (`x-fastapi-search: fulltext`) has a GIN index for
each entity with a search, only on Postgres. Make a migration that
changes these indexes with `DATABASE_URL` of a Postgres database:
`migrations/env.py` writes each GIN index under a test of the dialect,
so that the migration also runs on SQLite. On SQLite, Alembic does not
compare the indexes of an expression, and `q` finds each word with
`LIKE`.
{% endif %}

## Tests

The tests need a database. By default, they start Postgres in a container
with Testcontainers, so Docker must run. To use a database that runs
already, or SQLite, set `TEST_DATABASE_URL`, for example to
`sqlite+aiosqlite:///./test.db`. The tests delete all data in it.

Each operation has a test module in `tests/features/<slice>/`, with
the success case and the main error cases. The factories in
`tests/factories.py` give valid input and store records, with the
records that they need. The generated tests of a custom operation check
only its errors (404 and 422). Write the tests of its logic in your own
test modules, which appgen does not change.

## Docker

`docker compose up --build` builds the image, starts Postgres, runs the
migrations, and starts the API on port 8000 with the seed records
(`DATABASE_SEED=true`). The Dockerfile installs
the dependencies with uv in a build stage, and runs the API as a
non-root user. To use other images, for example from a mirror, set the
build arguments `UV_IMAGE` and `PYTHON_IMAGE`, and the environment
variable `POSTGRES_IMAGE` for Compose.

{% if py.jobs %}
## Jobs

The worker runs the jobs of the definition: `uv run python -m app.worker`
(Compose starts it as the service `worker`). Each
`JOBS_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (60), a pass runs each job at the current time,
in UTC.

| Job | When | What |
| --- | ---- | ---- |
{% for job in py.jobs.values() %}
| `{{ job.name }}` | {% if job.schedule %}The schedule `{{ job.schedule.expression }}`{% else %}The time of `{{ job.at.name }}`{{ " " ~ job.offset if job.offset != "0m" }}{% endif %} | {% if job.entity %}The operation `{{ job.operation.name }}` on each {{ job.entity.name }} record of the filter{% else %}`app/jobs/{{ job.module }}_ext.py`{% endif %} |
{% endfor %}

- A run is for one record (or for none) and one time. The table
  `job_runs` keeps each run with its state, and its key locks the run:
  each run runs one time, also with more than one worker.
- A pass runs only the times of the last `JOBS_WINDOW_MINUTES` (60).
  After a stop of the workers, the times before the window do not run.
- A failed run rolls back its changes, goes into the log with the job
  and the record, and runs again at the next pass while its time is in
  the window.
- `uv run python -m app.worker --once --now 2026-06-15T12:00:00Z` runs
  one pass at a fixed time. The tests of `tests/jobs/` use a fixed time
  too.
- `GET /health` on the port `WORKER_PORT` (8001) gives 200 while the
  passes run, and 503 when they stop. 0 turns the endpoint off.

{% endif %}
{% if py.events %}
## Events

The repositories run a custom operation after a change of a record, in
the transaction of the change (the `events` of the definition, and
`run` on a transition):

| Change | Operation |
| ------ | --------- |
{% for e in py.entity_order() %}
{% for ev in e.events %}
| {{ ev.summary(e.name) }} | `{{ ev.operation.name }}` |
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

- The operation runs after the change is in the database. An error of
  the operation rolls back the change. Until the operation has its
  logic in `<operation>_ext.py`, each change that starts it fails (501).
- In the operation, the repository functions do not commit, and a
  change starts no other event.
- The tests turn off the events, except the tests of
  `tests/features/<slice>/test_events.py` and the tests with the mark
  `events`.

{% endif %}
{% if py.history %}
## Change log

The repositories keep a change log of these entities in the table
`history_entries`: an entry for each create, change, and delete of a
record, with the user, the time, and the old and the new values.

| Entity | Fields | Endpoints |
| ------ | ------ | --------- |
{% for e in py.history %}
| {{ e.name }} | {% for member, _ in e.history.members %}`{{ member }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} | {% if e.history.read %}`GET {{ e.path }}/{id}/history`{% endif %}{% if e.history.read and e.history.update %}, {% endif %}{% if e.history.update %}`POST {{ e.path }}/{id}/history/{entry_id}/restore`{% endif %}{% if not e.history_endpoints %}None{% endif %} |
{% endfor %}

- A restore gives the fields of the entry of a change their old values
  back, with the checks of an update, and adds an entry `restored`.
- A change outside the repository functions adds no entry: SQL in a
  script, a delete of the database (`on_delete: cascade`), and the seed.

{% endif %}
{% if py.notifications %}
## Notices

A change of a record gives each user that it concerns a notice in the
table `notices`, except the user that made the change. The repositories
add the notices in the transaction of the change, after the events and
the change log.

| Notification | Change | Receivers | Text |
| ------------ | ------ | --------- | ---- |
{% for n in py.notifications %}
| `{{ n.name }}` | {{ n.summary() }} | {% for r in n.receivers %}`{{ r.path }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} | {{ n.text }} |
{% endfor %}

- `GET /notices` gives the notices of the signed-in user, the newest
  first, with the text in the language of the request. `unread=true`
  gives only the unread notices.
- `POST /notices/{id}/read` marks a notice read, and `POST /notices/read`
  marks all notices of the user read.
- A notice keeps the values of its text from the time of the change.
- A change outside the repository functions gives no notice: SQL in a
  script, and the seed.

{% endif %}
## Production

- Logs: the API writes one log line for each request, with the method,
  the path, the status, the duration, and a request ID (the
  `X-Request-ID` header, or a new ID). `LOG_JSON=true` (the default)
  writes each line as a JSON object, and `LOG_LEVEL` sets the lowest
  level.
- Rate limits: `RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE` sets the number of requests that
  each client address can send in each minute (0, the default, sets no
  limit). Over the limit, the API gives 429 with `Retry-After`. The
{% if py.redis %}
  counts are in the memory of each process, or in Redis with
  `RATE_LIMIT_STORE_URL` (for example `redis://localhost:6379/0`): then
  all replicas share one limit. When Redis is not available, the API
  logs a warning and lets the requests through, or gives 503 with
  `RATE_LIMIT_FAIL_CLOSED=true`. Compose starts Redis with
  `--profile redis`. `/health` has no limit.
{% else %}
  counts are in the memory of each process, so each replica has its own
  limit. `/health` has no limit.
{% endif %}
