## Sign-in

Each endpoint needs a bearer token (`Authorization: Bearer <token>`),
except `/health`, the sign-in{{ " and the sign-up" if definition.auth.sign_up }}, and the public
operations of the definition. `AUTH_SECRET` signs the tokens: set it to
a long random text in each environment.

{% if password %}
- `POST /auth/sign-in` gives a token for a user name and a password.
- `GET /auth/me` gives the signed-in user, and `PUT /auth/password`
  changes the password.
{% else %}
- `GET /auth/me` gives the signed-in user. The users sign in with a
  provider, not with a password.
{% endif %}
{% set verify = definition.auth.sign_up.verify_email if definition.auth.sign_up else none %}
{% if verify %}
- `POST /auth/sign-up` makes a new user with a password, and sends a
  message with a link that confirms the email address (202, without a
  token). A user name that exists gives 409. The user can sign in only
  after the user opens the link: before, the sign-in gives 403. `POST
  /auth/verify-email/confirm` confirms the address with the token of
  the link. A link is valid for {{ verify.token_minutes }} minutes. `POST /auth/verify-email`
  sends a new link, and gives 202 also for an unknown user name.
  `EMAIL_VERIFY_URL` is the page of the front end in the link.
{% elif definition.auth.sign_up %}
- `POST /auth/sign-up` makes a new user with a password, and gives a
  token. A user name that exists gives 409.
{% endif %}
{% if definition.auth.password_reset %}
- `POST /auth/password-reset` sends a reset link to a user, and gives
  202 also for an unknown user name. `POST /auth/password-reset/confirm`
  sets a new password with the token of the link. A link is valid for
  {{ definition.auth.password_reset.token_minutes }} minutes and one time. `PASSWORD_RESET_URL` is the page of
  the front end in the link.
{% endif %}
{% if py.smtp %}
- The app sends the messages with the links as emails with the SMTP
  server of `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`,
  `SMTP_FROM` (the sender), and `SMTP_TLS` (`starttls`, `tls`, or
  `none`). Without `SMTP_HOST`, the links go to the log with
  `ENVIRONMENT=development`, and the endpoints that send a message give
  501 in production.
{% elif py.message_elements %}
- Write the delivery of the messages with the links (for example an
  email) in `app/core/messages_ext.py`. Until then, the links go to the
  log with `ENVIRONMENT=development`, and the endpoints that send a
  message give 501 in production.
{% endif %}
{% if py.providers %}
- `GET /auth/providers/<name>/start` sends the browser to the sign-in
  page of a provider ({% for p in py.providers %}`{{ p.name }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}). The provider sends the browser
  back to `GET /auth/providers/<name>/callback`, which gives a token.
  With `?return_to=<page>` (a page of a front end in `CORS_ORIGINS`),
  the callback sends the browser to the page, with the token in the
  fragment of the URL (`#access_token=...`), or the error (`#error=...`).
  Register `<PUBLIC_URL>/auth/providers/<name>/callback` as the
  redirect URI of the app at the provider. Set the issuer, the client
  ID, and the client secret of each provider (see `.env.example`).
{% endif %}
{% set sf = definition.auth.second_factor if password else none %}
{% if sf %}
- The second factor (ADR 0024 of appgen): `POST /auth/second-factor/setup`
  gives a new secret and its `otpauth://` URI for an authenticator app,
  and `POST /auth/second-factor/confirm` with a code turns it on and
  gives 10 recovery codes one time. Then `POST /auth/sign-in` gives a
  challenge, not a token, and `POST /auth/sign-in/second-factor` with
  the challenge and a code, or a recovery code, gives the token.{% if sf.required_roles and definition.access %} The
  roles {% for name in sf.required_roles %}`{{ name }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} must use it: their first sign-in turns it on
  with the challenge.{% endif %} `GET /auth/second-factor` gives its state, and
  `POST /auth/second-factor/disable` with a code turns it off. After 5
  wrong codes in a row, the codes give 429 for 15 minutes. The app keeps
  the secret encrypted with a key from `AUTH_SECRET`: after a change of
  `AUTH_SECRET`, the codes of the authenticator apps are not valid. The
  users sign in with a recovery code, and turn the factor off and on.
- `uv run python -m app.cli disable-second-factor <user name>` turns off
  the second factor of a user who lost the authenticator app and the
  recovery codes.
{% endif %}
{% if password %}
- `uv run python -m app.cli set-password <user name>` sets the password
  of a user.

A new password needs {{ definition.auth.min_password_length }} or more characters.
{% endif %}

{% endif %}
## Layout

Each entity of the definition is a vertical slice: one folder with all
the code of the feature.

| Folder                  | Content                                              |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `app/main.py`           | The entry point. It adds the router of each slice.   |
| `app/extensions/`       | The hand-written routers (`routers_ext.py`).         |
| `app/models.py`         | Imports the models of all slices.                    |
| `app/core/`             | Code that all slices share: settings, database, errors, pagination, health, and schema bases. |
| `migrations/`           | The Alembic migrations.                              |
| `app/features/<slice>/` | One slice for each entity.                           |
{% if py.jobs %}
| `app/jobs/`             | One module for each job. A job without an entity has its logic in `<job>_ext.py`: write it there. |
| `app/worker.py`         | The worker that runs the jobs.                       |
{% endif %}
{% if py_apis %}
| `app/integrations/<api>/` | The client and the models of a third-party API, and the endpoints of an exposed API. |
{% endif %}
{% if py_webhooks %}
| `app/webhooks/<webhook>/` | The endpoint, the models of the body, and the logic of a webhook. |
{% endif %}
| `tests/`                | The tests, with one folder for each slice.           |

A slice has these modules:

| Module              | Content                                                     |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `router.py`         | The router of the slice. It adds the router of each operation. |
| `models.py`         | The SQLAlchemy model: the table, its columns, and relations. |
| `schemas.py`        | The Pydantic schemas: create, update, read, and the params of the custom operations. |
| `enums.py`          | The enums of the enum fields and params, if the entity has some. |
| `repository.py`     | The data access that the operations share.                  |
| `<operation>.py`    | One module for each CRUD or list operation: its endpoint and its handler. |
| `<operation>_gen.py` | The endpoint of a custom operation.                        |
| `<operation>_ext.py` | The logic of a custom operation. Write it here.            |
| `validation_ext.py` | The custom validation of the entity. Write it here.        |
{% if py.file_slices %}
| `files.py`          | The upload and the delete of the files of the file fields, if the entity has some. |
{% endif %}

