## Third-party APIs

Each API of the definition has a package in `app/integrations/`: the
Pydantic models of its schemas (`models.py`) and an async client with one
method for each operation that the app uses (`client.py`). An answer
with an error status raises `ApiError`, with the status and the body.
Each request stops after 10 seconds, unless the client gets another
`timeout`. The settings read the base URL and the secret from the
environment.

| API | Client | Base URL | Secret |
| --- | ------ | -------- | ------ |
{% for api in py_apis.values() %}
| `{{ api.name }}` | `app/integrations/{{ api.module }}/client.py` | `{{ api.url_env }}` | {{ ("`" ~ api.client_id_env ~ "` (client ID), ") if api.client_id_env }}{{ ("`" ~ api.secret_env ~ "`") if api.secret_env else "None" }} |
{% endfor %}

An endpoint gets a client as a dependency, for example
`client: {{ (py_apis.values() | first).dependency_type }}`. A custom operation with `apis` in the
definition gets its clients as arguments of its function in the
`_ext.py` file. The tests do not call the APIs: `tests/integrations/`
has a mock transport that answers with the examples of the OpenAPI
documents, and the tests of the operations get clients with it.
{% set exposed = py_apis.values() | selectattr("expose") | list %}
{% if exposed %}

### Endpoints for the front ends

The app has an endpoint for each operation of an exposed API
(`expose: true`), so that a front end can use the API without its
secret. The path of an endpoint is `/apis/<api>/<operation>` in kebab
case, for example `{{ exposed[0].prefix }}{{ exposed[0].methods[0].endpoint_path }}`, and
its method is the method of the operation. The endpoint takes the
parameters of the operation as query parameters, and the body of the
operation as its body. An error of the API gives 502.
{% if definition.auth %}
The endpoints need a signed-in user. The roles do not apply to them.
{% endif %}

| API | Endpoints |
| --- | --------- |
{% for api in exposed %}
| `{{ api.name }}` | `{{ api.prefix }}/...` in `app/integrations/{{ api.module }}/router.py` |
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if py_connections %}

### Connections of the users

The clients of {% for api in py_connections %}`{{ api.name }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} send the access token of a user: the
authorization code flow of OAuth 2.0. A user connects an account of the service first
(`app/core/connections.py`):

1. `POST /auth/connections/<api>` with `{"return_to": "<page>"}` gives
   the `authorization_url` for the signed-in user. Send the browser to
   it. `return_to` is a page of a front end in `CORS_ORIGINS`.
2. The user allows the access, and the service sends the browser back
   to `GET /auth/connections/<api>/callback`. The app changes the code
   for the tokens, and sends the browser to the page of `return_to` with
   `#connected=<api>`, or with `#error=<message>`.
3. `GET /auth/connections` lists the APIs and the connections of the
   user. `DELETE /auth/connections/<api>` deletes a connection.

Register `<PUBLIC_URL>/auth/connections/<api>/callback` as the redirect
URI of the app at the service. The table `api_connections` keeps the
tokens of each user, encrypted with a key from `AUTH_SECRET`: a new
`AUTH_SECRET` ends the connections. The client gets a new access token
with the refresh token when the token ends. An endpoint with the client
gives 409 if the signed-in user has no connection. Other code, for
example a job, gets a client with `<api>_client_for(settings, sessions,
user_id)`. With SQLite, a job cannot get a new access token: use
Postgres for the worker.
{% endif %}

{% endif %}
{% if py_webhooks %}
## Webhooks

Each webhook of the definition has a package in `app/webhooks/`: the
endpoint (`router.py`), the Pydantic models of the schema of the body
(`models.py`), and the logic (`handler_ext.py`). The endpoint checks the
signature of the raw body with the secret, and gives 401 for a wrong
signature. The table `webhook_events` keeps the ID of each event, so a
repeated delivery gives `"repeated": true` and does not run the logic
again. The row of the event and the changes of the logic are in one
transaction: when the logic fails, the sender can deliver the event
again.

| Webhook | Endpoint | Signature | Secret |
| ------- | -------- | --------- | ------ |
{% for w in py_webhooks.values() %}
| `{{ w.name }}` | `POST {{ w.path }}` | {{ w.signature_text }} | `{{ w.secret_env }}` |
{% endfor %}
{% if py_webhooks.values() | selectattr("email") | list %}

A webhook with the format `email` receives the inbound emails that a
mail service forwards (Mailgun, Postmark, or SendGrid). The logic gets
the email as `app.core.inbound_email.InboundEmail`: the sender, the receivers, the subject, the
text, the `In-Reply-To` header, and the names and sizes of the
attachments. The event ID is the `Message-ID` header. With HTTP Basic,
the secret is `<user name>:<password>`, and the URL of the webhook at
the mail service has them, for example
`https://<user name>:<password>@example.com/webhooks/support`.
{% endif %}

Without its secret, an endpoint gives 500. The tests in `tests/webhooks/`
sign the requests with a test secret, and replace the logic with a
function that records the events.

{% endif %}
## Errors and pages

All errors use the problem details format of RFC 9457, with the media
type `application/problem+json`. A request with values that are not
valid gives 422, with one item for each error. A record that does not
exist gives 404. A change that breaks a unique value or a reference gives
409. A custom operation without logic gives 501.

List endpoints take `limit` (default 50), `offset`, and `sort`. The
`sort` value is a list of sort keys, separated by commas. A `-` before
a key sorts in descending order, for example `sort=-due_date,title`.
Empty values come last. The sort keys are the fields with a simple type,
and the sortable columns and the sort of the views, for example `owner`
(the display field of the related record) or `project.name`.
List endpoints also take one equality filter for each field with a
simple type, for example `done=true`. The filters of the views add more
parameters:

| Filter of a view | Parameters | Example |
| ---------------- | ---------- | ------- |
| `select`         | The field takes one or more values. | `status=todo&status=done` |
| `boolean`        | The field takes `true` or `false`. | `active=true` |
| `date_range`     | `<field>_from` and `<field>_to`: the first and the last day. | `due_date_from=2026-01-01` |
| `search`         | `q`: each word is in one of the fields of the search filters. | `q=alpha` |

A field path of a view keeps its dots, for example `project.status=active`.
A tab of a detail view that lists the records of a many-to-many relation
adds `id`, a list of record IDs, to the list endpoint of the target.
An unknown parameter, value, or sort key gives 422. The response has the
`items` and the `total` number of records.

{% if i18n %}
## Languages

The API gives its messages in the language of the request: the first
language of the `Accept-Language` header that the API has, by its code
or its locale, also with only the same primary language (`de-CH` gives
`de`), else the default language (`{{ i18n.default }}`). The languages are
{% for language in i18n.languages %}`{{ language.code }}` ({{ language.label }}){{ ", " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %}


Each response has the locale of the language in the header
`Content-Language`. The title and the detail of the problem details,
and the message of each error of the input, are in the language. The
translations come from the `translations` section of the definition
(`app/core/i18n.py`). Use `t` of `app.core.i18n` for a message of an
extension file, for example `ProblemError(422, t("The room is closed."))`,
and add its translation to the definition.

{% if py.translatable %}
### Values in each language

These fields have a value in each language:

{% for e in py.entity_order() %}
{% for f in e.translatable %}
- `{{ e.name }}.{{ f.name }}`
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

A read gives the value of the language of the request, else the value
of the default language. With the query parameter `translations=all`, a
read also gives the values of all languages in `<field>_translations`.
A create or a change sets the value of the language of the request, and
`<field>_translations` sets the value of each language that it gives.
The table keeps the value of the default language in the column of the
field, so the search, the sort, and the filters use it.

{% endif %}
