## Files

{% if st.s3 %}
The app keeps the files of the file fields in an S3-compatible service:
Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, MinIO, and others. Set the bucket in
`{{ st.variables["bucket_env"] }}`, and for a service that is not Amazon S3 its URL in
`{{ st.variables["endpoint_env"] }}`. The keys are in `{{ st.variables["access_key_env"] }}` and
`{{ st.variables["secret_key_env"] }}`; without them, boto3 uses the AWS configuration of
the machine, for example its role. Without a bucket, the app keeps the
files in the folder of `{{ st.variables["folder_env"] }}` (default `uploads`), for example
on your machine and in the tests.
{% else %}
The app keeps the files of the file fields in the folder of
`{{ st.variables["folder_env"] }}` (default `uploads`). The Docker image has the folder
`/app/uploads`, and `compose.yaml` keeps it in a volume.
{% endif %}

{% for e in py.file_entities %}
{% for f in e.files %}
- `{{ e.name }}.{{ f.name }}`: at most {{ f.size_text }}{% if f.media_types %}, the types {% for t in f.media_types %}`{{ t }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}{% endif %}.
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

`PUT <path>/{id}/<field>` uploads the file of a record, with a multipart
body that has the file in the part `file`. A new file replaces the old
one. `DELETE <path>/{id}/<field>` deletes the file. Both need the
permission of the update operation of the entity, else of its create
operation, and return the record. A file that is too large gives 413, and
a type that the field does not allow gives 415. The app takes the type
from the first bytes of a PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, or PDF file, else from
the part.

A read gives each file as `name`, `size`, `type`, and `url`. The URL is
a signed link (`/file-links/<token>`) that works without a sign-in, so
an `<img src>` shows an image, until it ends after `STORAGE_LINK_SECONDS`
(default 3600). Set `STORAGE_LINK_SECRET` to a long random text, the same
in all processes; without it, each process signs its own links. The
links start with `PUBLIC_URL`, else with the URL of the request.

The delete of a record deletes its files. The files of the records that
the database deletes with `on_delete: cascade` stay in the storage.

{% endif %}
